For a single rule that covers everything, start here. The default rule applies at all levels: catalogs, schemas, and tables. Any asset with a matching property is excluded from indexing.

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "default_rule": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

With this rule in place, setting `iomete.governance.index=false` on any catalog, schema, or table excludes it from the sync. One rule, applied wherever you need it via properties.

For catalog-level control, exclude catalogs by name, by property, or both.

Exclusion Rules › By Name
List catalog names directly. These catalogs are always skipped, regardless of their properties.

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "catalogs": {
      "names": ["sandbox_dev", "legacy_warehouse", "user_personal_spaces"]
    }
  }
}
```

Set `iomete.governance.index` to `false` on the catalog in the Admin Portal when you create or edit it.

Then add the matching filter:

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "catalogs": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Use names and properties together. A catalog is excluded if it matches either condition.

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "catalogs": {
      "names": ["sandbox_dev", "legacy_warehouse"],
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

To keep a catalog in the index but skip a few schemas inside it, use namespace exclusion. It's property-based. Set the property in SQL to mark a namespace for exclusion:

```sql
ALTER DATABASE my_catalog.my_schema SET DBPROPERTIES ('iomete.governance.index' = 'false');
```

Verify the property:

```sql
DESCRIBE DATABASE EXTENDED my_catalog.my_schema;
```

To re-enable indexing later, flip the property back:

```sql
ALTER DATABASE my_catalog.my_schema SET DBPROPERTIES ('iomete.governance.index' = 'true');
```

Then add the exclusion rule:

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "schemas": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Tables work the same way as namespaces. Set a property on the table, then define the matching rule.

```sql
ALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES ('iomete.governance.index' = 'false');
```

Verify the property:

```sql
SHOW TBLPROPERTIES my_catalog.my_schema.my_table;
```

To remove the property later:

```sql
ALTER TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table UNSET TBLPROPERTIES ('iomete.governance.index');
```

Then add the exclusion rule:

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "tables": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

When a table exclusion rule lists multiple properties, a table is excluded if any of them match.

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "tables": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false",
        "hidden": "true"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

A table is excluded if `iomete.governance.index` is `false` or `hidden` is `true`. It doesn't need to match both.

A complete configuration that uses every exclusion level:

```json
{
  "exclusion_rules": {
    "catalogs": {
      "names": ["sandbox_dev", "legacy_warehouse", "user_personal_spaces"],
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    },
    "schemas": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    },
    "tables": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false",
        "hidden": "true"
      }
    },
    "default_rule": {
      "filter_by_properties": {
        "iomete.governance.index": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The job deletes folders only under one location per database, the cleanup root. It chooses the cleanup root from the database's table locations: when the tables share one parent folder, that folder is the cleanup root; otherwise the job uses the database location. The cleanup root must stay inside the database location, or the same path without the `.db` suffix.

When tables are stored under more than one parent folder, table locations outside the cleanup root are not checked in that run. The job reports them under `TABLE LOCATIONS OUTSIDE THE SCANNED ROOT`, and a successful audit row gets `status_reason=partial_scan_coverage`.

The job also lists other roots one level deep for review. Nothing in these report-only roots is ever deleted. A root is listed when it sits inside the database's own storage:

- the database location, or the same path without the `.db` suffix;
- for `spark_catalog` and IOMETE-managed REST catalogs, the catalog's namespace folder for the database;
- a location you approve in `report_only_storage_roots`, as long as at least one visible table of the database is stored under it.

```json
{
  "report_only_storage_roots": {
    "sales": ["s3a://other-bucket/external/sales_events"]
  }
}
```

Use `exclude_paths` when you know the full object-storage path to protect. Each entry must include a scheme and bucket:

```json
{
  "exclude_paths": [
    "s3a://bucket/data/analytics/manual_archive"
  ]
}
```

Use `exclude_database_folders` when you want to protect a folder relative to a configured database:

```json
{
  "exclude_database_folders": [
    "analytics.manual_archive"
  ]
}
```

A folder is skipped when it equals an excluded path, contains one, or sits inside one. Paths are compared by whole path segments, so `manual_archive` does not protect `manual_archive_v2`. A `database.folder` exclusion also applies to the same folder name under every report-only root of that database.

Both forms are included in the effective excluded-path list written to the audit table.

Keeps 7 days of snapshots everywhere, but 30 days for `analytics.user_events`, and rewrites that table sorted by `user_id`. `rewrite-all` rewrites every file, not just small ones, so the whole table ends up sorted.

```hocon
{
    catalog: "spark_catalog",
    expire_snapshot: {
        older_than_days: 7
    },
    table_overrides: {
        analytics.user_events: {
            expire_snapshot: {
                older_than_days: 30
            },
            rewrite_data_files: {
                strategy: "sort",
                sort_order: "user_id ASC",
                options: {
                    "rewrite-all": true
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Expires snapshots and removes orphan files, but skips both rewrite operations.

```hocon
{
    catalog: "spark_catalog",
    rewrite_data_files: {
        enabled: false
    },
    rewrite_manifests: {
        enabled: false
    }
}
```

Open Streaming Jobs in the sidebar and click  if you do not use the Marketplace flow.

1. Name and Application

- Name: any name you like, for example `kafka-iceberg-stream`
- Application type: `Python`
- Docker image: `iomete.azurecr.io/iomete/kafka-iceberg-stream:1.2.0` (replace with the latest version)
- Main application file: `local:///app/job.py`
- Java options (optional): `-Dlog4j.configurationFile=/opt/spark/iomete/log4j2.properties`, specify a logging configuration file

2. Config File

Expand Configurations, select the Config Maps tab, click Add config, and paste the HOCON template below. See Configuration Properties for what each field does.

```hocon
{
  kafka: {
    options: {
      "kafka.bootstrap.servers": "kafka-bootstrap-server:9092",
      "subscribePattern": ".*"
    },

    # either once or processing_time should be set for the trigger. Not both.
    trigger: {
      # processing_time: "15 minutes",
      once: True
    },

    # set checkpoint_location to object storage for production.
    # Example: "s3a://assets-dir/checkpoints/kafka-streaming/data/app1"
    checkpoint_location: ".tmp/checkpoints/kafka-streaming/data/app1",
  },
  destination: {
    database: "default",
    table: "all_db_changes_v1",
  }
}
```

Create a separate job template for restores so a scheduled backup cannot accidentally run with reversed locations. Configure the backup location as `source` and the restore location as `target`, then run it while the restore target is not being written by another process.

For example, restore an HDFS backup to S3 with:

```json
{
  "source": {
    "type": "hdfs",
    "namenode": "isilon.example.com:8020",
    "path": "backups/lakehouse",
    "user": "iomete-backup"
  },
  "target": {
    "type": "s3",
    "bucket": "",
    "prefix": "",
    "accessKey": "$",
    "secretKey": "$"
  }
}
```

After the run succeeds, validate the restored catalog or workload against the copied files before directing production traffic to it.

Brings over only new or changed rows in the `salaries` table, using `id` as the primary key and `updated_at` as the change-tracking column.

```hocon
{
    source.schema: employees
    source.tables: [salaries]
    destination.schema: employees_raw
    sync_mode: {
      type: incremental_snapshot
      identification_column: id
      tracking_column: updated_at
    }
}
```

You can also use named references (branches and tags) to query data at a specific time.
In the example below we will create a tag and then use it to query data.

```sql
-- create tag
ALTER TABLE default.time_travel_example CREATE TAG sample_tag;

-- View all references (branches and tags)
SELECT * FROM default.time_travel_example.refs;

-- time travel to tag / branch
SELECT * FROM spark_catalog.default.time_travel_example VERSION AS OF 'sample_tag';
SELECT * FROM spark_catalog.default.time_travel_example VERSION AS OF 'main';
```

More about branches and tags in the What's next section below (Soon...).

Session-scoped variables declared and set in SQL, usable in any later query of the same session.

```sql
DECLARE VARIABLE min_amount DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 600.00;
SET VARIABLE min_amount = (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM sales);

SELECT region, product, amount FROM sales WHERE amount > min_amount;

DROP TEMPORARY VARIABLE min_amount;
```

Run dynamically composed SQL, optionally binding parameters and capturing the result into a session variable.

```sql
EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sales';

DECLARE VARIABLE cnt INT;
EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sales' INTO cnt;
SELECT cnt;
```

Procedural blocks with variables, conditionals and loops (`BEGIN ... END`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `FOR`).

```sql
BEGIN
  DECLARE total DECIMAL(10,2);
  SET total = (SELECT SUM(amount) FROM sales);
  IF total > 4000 THEN
    SELECT concat('high: ', total) AS verdict;
  ELSE
    SELECT concat('low: ', total) AS verdict;
  END IF;
END
```

Format v3 tables track each row's identity and last modification via the `_row_id` and `_last_updated_sequence_number` metadata columns.

```sql
SELECT id, region, amount, _row_id, _last_updated_sequence_number
FROM sales ORDER BY id;

UPDATE sales SET amount = amount + 1 WHERE region = 'EU';
-- EU rows keep their _row_id but get a higher _last_updated_sequence_number;
-- all other rows are unchanged. Fresh inserts get new _row_id values.
```

With merge-on-read, format v3 tables store deletes as compact deletion vectors (Puffin files) instead of rewriting data files.

```sql
ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES (
  'write.delete.mode'='merge-on-read',
  'write.update.mode'='merge-on-read',
  'write.merge.mode'='merge-on-read'
);

DELETE FROM sales WHERE id IN (2, 4);

SELECT content, file_format, record_count FROM spark_catalog.default.sales.delete_files;
-- file_format = PUFFIN  (deletion vectors, not v2 delete files)
```

